jit++ is a cooperative tracing jit written in c++ that compiles x86-64 assembly into x86-64 assembly using user placed callsites as monitored hotspots.

(c) 2008 Edward Kmett
All Rights Reserved

Single-module build (Linux x86-64)
=================================

The C++26 interface is one named module, jitpp. It exports jitpp::interpreter
and jitpp::application. The interpreter is a single concrete class. Its
declaration belongs to the global module fragment, preserving the C++ symbols
used by the assembly trampoline; the module exports it with a using-declaration,
following native's native.isa provider pattern.

Use CMake 3.30 or newer, Ninja, and Clang 19 or newer with its matching
clang-scan-deps (packaged as clang-tools-19 on Debian/Ubuntu). Clang 19 is tested.
Install glog, gflags, and udis86 as described below, then:

    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja \
        -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++-19 -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
        -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$PWD/build-deps"
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

Use -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug for an unoptimized build. CMake generates its own
configuration header; running Autotools first is unnecessary. Exceptions stay
enabled because unsupported instructions use them to return to native execution.

An enclosing CMake project can consume the module with:

    add_subdirectory(path/to/jitplusplus)
    add_executable(example main.cc)
    set_target_properties(example PROPERTIES CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF)
    target_link_libraries(example PRIVATE jitpp::jitpp)

Then main.cc can use `import jitpp;` without including jit++ headers. CMake
builds the module and propagates the C++26 requirement. Consumers must match
the provider's strict C++ mode (CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF). The module build runs
the same round-trip and demo tests as the header build. To run its benchmark:

    cmake --build build --target bench_interpreter
    ./build/bench_interpreter

Third-Party Dependencies:

udis86				-- use for debugging assembly 
google logging framework (glog) -- used to show output
google flags (gflags)           -- used to pass options to the tracer
libtool				-- used by the build process
    boost unit test framework 1.36	-- used by the test suite

Resurrection baseline (Linux x86-64)
===================================

The original interpreter and assembly entry/exit trampoline build with modern
GCC. This is an experimental interpreter foundation, not a completed optimizing
JIT. Unsupported instructions return to native execution at the original PC;
modern libc commonly triggers this path on AVX instructions.

On Debian/Ubuntu, install the build prerequisites:

    sudo apt-get install build-essential autoconf automake libtool git python3 \
        libgoogle-glog-dev libgflags-dev

udis86 is used for diagnostic disassembly. If it is not already installed, the
helper builds a pinned upstream revision locally, fixing its Python 3 integer
division compatibility:

    sh bin/build-udis86.sh "$PWD/build-deps"
    export CPPFLAGS="-I$PWD/build-deps/include"
    export LDFLAGS="-L$PWD/build-deps/lib -Wl,-rpath,$PWD/build-deps/lib"

Bootstrap, build, and run the tests:

    sh bin/autogen.sh

Or run the stages individually:

    autoreconf -fi
    ./configure
    make -j2
    make check

The active tests do not require Boost. test_interpreter runs the original demo;
test_roundtrip uses a deterministic assembly sequence to check interpreted
arithmetic, stores, arithmetic flags on native resumption, repeated entry,
step-limited exit, and unsupported-instruction fallback. The assertions remain
active in optimized builds. For instruction logging:

    ./test_interpreter --logtostderr --v=1

The concrete interpreter owns its registers, decoder, flags, and instruction
methods directly, with no inheritance, virtual dispatch, or PIMPL allocation.
Compile-time layout checks pin the offsets consumed by assembly. The round-trip
test inspects saved execution state and exercises arithmetic and locked memory
operations across the instruction groups.

To measure dispatch/state-access throughput without worker-thread startup:

    make bench_interpreter
    ./bench_interpreter

The benchmark reports the median of nine runs of a million-iteration add/dec/jnz
loop. Use the same optimized build flags and logging settings when comparing runs.

The original design still uses a worker pthread and a fixed gap on the caller's
stack. Instruction coverage, syscall/TLS behavior, and extended vector state are
incomplete; the round-trip tests establish a baseline, not general x86-64
correctness. The old Boost tests remain disabled.

Existing Autotools deprecation warnings and return-type warnings in the legacy
flags helpers remain; those paths need further correctness work.
